WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 79: The Dahlonega Gold Rush: America's Forgotten First Gold Fever

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.960
[Rebecca] Long before the 49ers raced across the plains to California, America's first great gold rush erupted in the misty blue ridges of North Georgia.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:22.320
[Rebecca] In 1828, a deer hunter tripped over a rock containing gleaming yellow veins of pure gold in the Appalachian foothills.

5
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:31.440
[Rebecca] Within months, over 15,000 frenzied prospectors, known as the Great Intrusion, flooded into the sovereign territory of the Cherokee Nation.

6
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:46.880
[Rebecca] The discovery minted millions in gold coin, led to the creation of an official United States branch mint in Dalunga, and sealed the tragic political fate of the Cherokee people, directly sparking the Indian Removal Act and the Trail of Tears.

7
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:54.000
[Rebecca] George brings us the dramatic, poignant story of the Dalunga Gold Rush.

8
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:07.280
[George] In the late autumn of 1828, a humble frontier deer hunter named Benjamin Parks was tracking deer through the pine and hardwood forests of Hall County in northern Georgia, near the headwaters of the Chestate River.

9
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:16.880
[George] Pausing to catch his breath on a wooded hillside, Parks's boot dislodged a heavy stone that caught the morning sunlight with an unmistakable, brilliant yellow luster.

10
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:23.520
[George] Prying the stone from the loam with his hunting knife, Parks held a rock dense with veins of pure native gold.

11
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.920
[George] Parks later recalled that the stone looked as yellow as the yolk of an egg.

12
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:39.360
[George] While minor traces of placer gold had been panned in North Carolina decades earlier, the Georgia Appalachian Mountains sat atop the richest load of vein and placer gold in eastern North America.

13
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:53.520
[George] Parks showed the specimen to a local merchant, and despite sworn promises of secrecy, the explosive news leaked across the frontier like wildfire, igniting the first feverish mineral rush in the history of the United States.

14
00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:07.320
[George] Benjamin Parks was hunting in the rugged ridges between the Chestate and Etoa rivers when he found the legendary golden nugget, completely unaware that his discovery would ignite a political firestorm that would reshape the map of the American South.

15
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:21.160
[George] The gold deposits in Lumpkin County were extraordinarily rich, placer gold washed down mountain creeks in coarse yellow flakes, while underground quartz veins contained dense nuggets of 95% pure natural gold.

16
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.760
[George] The fatal tragedy of the discovery was its geography.

17
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:31.240
[George] The richest deposits lay squarely inside the legally recognized, sovereign borders of the Cherokee Nation.

18
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:45.800
[George] Under federal treaties signed by George Washington and John Adams, Cherokee Land was inviolable territory where white settlement was strictly prohibited by federal law, but the golden fever overwhelmed all treaties and moral boundaries.

19
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:58.920
[George] By the spring of 1829, over 15,000 unruly, armed white prospectors, gamblers and speculators flooded into the Cherokee Mountains in an illegal wave that historians call the Great Intrusion.

20
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:09.960
[George] Men abandoned farms, shops, and families across Virginia, the Carolinas and Tennessee, traveling by foot and horseback with picks, pans and sluice boxes.

21
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:18.200
[George] Shantytowns of canvas tents, log groggeries, and gambling shacks sprouted overnight along Duke's Creek and the Yahula River.

22
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:29.800
[George] Unscrupulous intruders tore up Cherokee cornfields, diverted sacred river waters to wash gravel, and violently assaulted Cherokee families who attempted to defend their sovereign soil.

23
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:41.800
[George] The illegal flood of thousands of white intruders completely overwhelmed the fragile frontier legal system, as squatters established lawless mining camps with colorful names like Scrabble Town and Auraria.

24
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:55.280
[George] Georgia authorities turned a blind eye to the widespread destruction of Cherokee farms and orchards, eager to exploit the gold discovery as an excuse to extinguish Cherokee sovereignty and claim the mineral wealth for the state treasury.

25
00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:10.480
[George] The Cherokee Nation was not a primitive nomadic tribe, but a highly sophisticated, literate, Christian constitutional republic, guided by Principal Chief John Ross and brilliant statesman Sekwoya, who had invented the Cherokee syllabary.

26
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:21.440
[George] Giving his people a written language and a national newspaper, the Cherokee Phoenix, the Cherokee had established an elected parliament, written laws, and successful plantations.

27
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:32.960
[George] Chief Ross appealed directly to the federal government in Washington, demanding that President Andrew Jackson enforce federal treaties and dispatch federal troops to eject the illegal gold squatters.

28
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:49.600
[George] Instead, the Georgia state legislature enacted draconian laws in 1830, declaring all Cherokee laws null and void, prohibiting Cherokee people from testifying against white men in court, and making it a crime for any Cherokee to dig gold on their own ancestral land.

29
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.560
[George] The Cherokee took their fight to the Supreme Court.

30
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.680
[George] In the landmark 1832 case Worcester v.

31
00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:04.720
[George] Georgia, Chief Justice John Marshall ruled emphatically that the Cherokee Nation was a distinct sovereign community in which the laws of Georgia had no force.

32
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:13.280
[George] But President Jackson notoriously defied the court, declaring John Marshall has made his decision, now let him enforce it.

33
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:29.720
[George] Chief John Ross was a wealthy, educated mixed-blood Cherokee statesman who fought tirelessly in the halls of Congress and the federal courts to defend his people's treaty rights, hiring premier constitutional lawyers like William Wirt to represent the nation before the Supreme Court.

34
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:35.480
[George] The refusal of President Andrew Jackson to enforce John Marshall's Supreme Court ruling in Worcester v.

35
00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:45.000
[George] Georgia precipitated a grave constitutional crisis, undermining the authority of the federal judiciary to appease Southern land and gold speculators.

36
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:56.680
[George] With the federal government refusing to intervene, the state of Georgia executed one of the most brazen land confiscations in American history, the Georgia Gold Lottery of 1832.

37
00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:05.240
[George] State surveyors divided the ancestral Cherokee Mountain Territory into 40-acre gold lots and 160-acre landlots.

38
00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:13.240
[George] Georgia citizens paid a nominal registration fee of $4 to enter their names into lottery drums in Milledgeville, the state capital.

39
00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:24.520
[George] Over several weeks, state officials drew tickets, awarding legal titles to prime Cherokee farms, orchards and gold mines to white citizens who had never set foot in the mountains.

40
00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:35.960
[George] Cherokee families, including wealthy leaders who lived in fine brick mansions and owned gristmills, were forced out of their ancestral homes at gunpoint by lottery winners clutching paper deeds.

41
00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:50.920
[George] In 1835, a small, unauthorized faction of desperate Cherokees signed the fraudulent Treaty of New Ecota, agreeing to surrender all eastern lands in exchange for territory in modern day Oklahoma, sealing the doom of their nation.

42
00:06:50.920 --> 00:07:03.640
[George] The 1832 gold lottery was an unprecedented spectacle of state sanctioned dispossession, as thousands of white citizens traveled to Milledgeville to watch officials draw numbered tickets from spinning wooden drums.

43
00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:19.600
[George] Lottery winners arrived in the Cherokee Mountains with armed sheriffs's possess to demand immediate possession of productive farms and gold mines, forcing Cherokee families who had lived on the land for generations to pack what few belongings they could carry and flee into the surrounding woods.

44
00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:27.680
[George] In the center of the gold fields arose the boomton of Dalonga, named from the Cherokee word Dalonga, meaning pure yellow.

45
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:36.560
[George] Dalonga transformed from a rowdy shantytown into a prosperous mountain city with a handsome brick courthouse, bank, and substantial hotels.

46
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:45.520
[George] Gold was so plentiful that local commerce functioned entirely on gold dust, with merchants keeping balanced scales beside their cash boxes.

47
00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:57.440
[George] Between 1830 and 1848, miners extracted an estimated 600,000 ounces of gold from North Georgia mines like the Calhoun, the Consolidated, and the Loud Mine.

48
00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:12.560
[George] To accommodate the immense flow of precious metal and eliminate the dangerous journey of transporting raw gold to the Philadelphia Mint, Congress authorized the construction of the Dalonga branch mint in 1835, opening in 1838.

49
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:28.640
[George] The handsome neoclassical facility stamped over $6 million in magnificent gold coins bearing the coveted D mint mark, including $5 half-eagles and tiny $1 gold pieces that remain among the rarest and most prized numismatic treasures in the world.

50
00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:38.960
[George] The Dalonga Mint was a masterpiece of neoclassical architecture, designed by renowned architect William Strickland and constructed of native Georgia granite and brick.

51
00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:54.720
[George] During its 23 years of continuous operation, the mint produced more than 1,300,000 gold coins, each stamped with the historic D mint mark that today commands astronomical sums among international coin collectors and historical institutions.

52
00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:00.000
[George] The dark culmination of the gold rush unfolded in the bitter autumn and winter of 1830.

53
00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:19.480
[George] Under orders from President Martin Van Buren, General Winfield Scott and 7,000 Federal troops rounded up 16,000 Cherokee men, women, and children at Bayonet Point, herding them into disease-ridden stockades before marching them over a thousand miles on foot to Indian territory.

54
00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:30.680
[George] Over 4,000 Cherokee people died of starvation, dysentery, exposure, and broken hearts along the horrific journey known forever in historical memory as the Trail of Tears.

55
00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:36.200
[George] Just as the Cherokee were expelled, a sudden shift in mineral fortune struck Dalonga.

56
00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.160
[George] In 1848, gold was discovered at Sutter's Mill in California.

57
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:47.880
[George] Almost overnight, thousands of experienced Georgia miners packed their tools and abandoned Dalonga to head west.

58
00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:53.400
[George] Standing on the courthouse steps in Dalonga in 1849, Mint asked your doctor.

59
00:09:53.400 --> 00:10:02.040
[George] Matthew Stephenson pleaded with miners to stay, pointing to the surrounding hills and shouting the famous words Why go to California?

60
00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.640
[George] There's millions in it.

61
00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:06.040
[George] There's gold in them thar hills.

62
00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:11.880
[George] But the lure of California was irresistible, and the Georgia gold boom collapsed into memory.

63
00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:25.320
[George] The forced march of the Cherokee on the Trail of Tears remains one of the darkest stains in American history, as thousands of innocent people succumbed to freezing blizzards and epidemic disease along the forced migration route to Oklahoma.

64
00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:40.520
[George] When California gold was discovered at Sutter's Mill in 1848, thousands of experienced Georgia miners joined the rush, carrying their sophisticated mountain mining techniques to the Pacific coast and creating California's famous gold boom.

65
00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:54.560
[George] The Dalonga Mint closed forever in 1861 with the outbreak of the Civil War, and the building later burned to the ground, though its foundational walls now support Price Memorial Hall on the campus of the University of North Georgia.

66
00:10:54.560 --> 00:11:01.360
[George] Yet the legacy of America's first gold rush remains etched into the landscape and cultural memory of the region.

67
00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:18.880
[George] In 1958, citizens of Dalonga loaded 43 ounces of pure locally panned gold onto an authentic horse-drawn wagon train, transporting the gold to Atlanta to gild the exterior of the towering Georgia State Capitol Dome, which gleams with Dalonga gold to this day.

68
00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:28.400
[George] Today, Dalonga is a charming mountain town where visitors pan for gold in rushing mountain streams and tour the deep shafts of the consolidated gold mine.

69
00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:42.080
[George] But beneath the sparkling tourist allure lies the solemn memory of a mountain paradise whose golden curse forever displaced a noble native nation, a powerful reminder of the human cost of America's unquenchable thirst for gold.

70
00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:53.200
[George] The dazzling gold leaf adorning the Georgia State Capitol Dome stands as a glittering architectural symbol of the Dalonga Gold Rush, visible for miles across downtown Atlanta.

71
00:11:53.200 --> 00:12:10.080
[George] Today, Dalonga celebrates its rich mining heritage with annual festivals, gold panning competitions, and historic tour, honoring the miners who opened the frontier while solemnly remembering the Cherokee families whose ancestral home was sacrificed on the altar of gold.

72
00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:19.360
[Rebecca] The Dalonga Gold Rush brought untold wealth and heartbreaking tragedy, forever intertwining the lure of fortune with the sorrow of the Trail of Tears.

73
00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:20.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

74
00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:28.480
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we investigate the mystery of America's first English settlement, the lost colony of Roanoke.

